TEST 13553 -- QoS: switchport interface - wrr weightings applied
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-24 ~16:10-16:15 NZST, tb470
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20
DUT:    IE520 3-member VCStack "IE520-stk"
Setup:  same topology and measurement as 13549.log (same session): two ~983 Mbps tagged
        streams converge on port3.0.13, counted per source MAC on eth1 (0 kernel drops)
        and on the DUT's egress queue counters.
        eth3 -> port3.0.9 frames carry PCP 5 (-> Q5); eth2 -> SA -> sa3 frames carry PCP 4 (-> Q4).
        (Replaces the 2026-09-22 UNMEASURED "requires congestion".)

VERDICT: PASS -- the received ratio between the two WRR queues matches the configured weights.

DEVIATIONS FROM THE CASE TEXT (both forced by the platform)
  - The case assigns CoS with `mls qos cos 5` / `mls qos cos 4` on untagged ingress ports.
    On this build a port's default CoS does not change the queue of its untagged traffic
    (13549.log NOTES, CAMPAIGN-QUEUE I-17), so the CoS is carried in the 802.1p tag instead.
  - The case's interface command `wrr-queue weight <w> queues <q>` does not exist on the
    IE520 (`% Invalid input` at "queues"). WRR is set through a scheduler-set:
      mls qos scheduler-set 1 wrr-queue group 1 weight <6-255> queues 5
      mls qos scheduler-set 1 wrr-queue group 1 weight <6-255> queues 4
      interface port3.0.13 / mls qos scheduler-set 1
    show mls qos interface port3.0.13 -> "Scheduler-set: 1".
    (`show mls qos scheduler-set 1` answers "% Invalid input".)

